 go inside the tumor  the outside you can see net gene  um heavily expressed within the tumor we  can see  um the hla dra gene expressed  in the tumor and then across the  boundary  um you know of course chemokines are not  highly expressed within the tumor  but then mhc class ii molecules are  highly expressed  along the border but then decrease the  farther away  that you get so you really get to see in  this particular roi  of the changes across the boundary and  how the biology is  how the tumor is interacting with the  immune environment  um and then the great thing about the  dhp platform is built for large cohorts  of samples so here we have  um two different patients one is um  the highly infiltrated tumor and the  other one is the immune excluded tumor  but this system is really designed to  have large cohorts whether they're tmas  or you're running  24 or 48 slides through the system you  can take all that data and put it  together  so here we're looking at you know the  differences in the immune response of  cd8s and cd3  t cells across the immune boundary um  and each dot here is a different um  measure at a different point along the  boundary um  we can see things like um you know in  this case  um the inflammatory macrophages  are enriched in the  inflamed tumor with the heavily  infiltrated  uh t cells uh we can see that distinct  difference here  and you know this kind of shows us that  there's a  strong m1 and m2 macrophage polarization  um with that particular patient  that has heavily infiltrated um  [Music]  immune cells into the tumor  and we can also take this data and we  could you know apply pathway  analysis to it so you can take things  like you know an  interfering signaling pathway and you  can basically  measure it across the border and what  we're seeing here is you know we've  taken many uh tumor stroma  boundaries across the whole tissue and  you know each line here  may it could be a different boundary  so there are other things that we can  ask with these particular samples  we you know maybe we want to know how  the t cell functions  are different depending on if it's a  infiltrated tumor versus one that has a  strong boundary  and so we can do that um here we've  selected regions that are inside the  tumor  and we've selected regions that are  outside the tumor so we want to know how  the t cells are changing if they're able  to get inside the tumor  you know how are they able to overcome  this barrier  and why are you know some t cells kind  of hanging outside  the tumor boundary and so what we're  able  to do is basically  select all the t cells and then we can  select everything that  neighbors a t cell right so you get both  this profile for what is happening in  the t cells  and then you can see how it's actually  affecting the environment around it  and so we can see things obviously um  you know just as a  um control your cd3 is highly expressed  in  t cells obviously and low league spread  see in the neighbor  compartment but then you have um you  know things like  um tim3 which is upregulated in t  cells within the tumor but not so much  in the stromal environment when the  t-cells in the stromal environment  same with 41 bb so you kind of pick  apart the different  uh t-cell profiles for those that are  inside the tumor versus outside of the  tumor  and and this is just some follow-up  experiments that we did with  interferon gamma on an rna scope to show  you know that inflamed phenotype  and so the last thing i want to show you  is um  just an example that  [Music]  that a customer did for a conference  earlier this year and so one thing that  we found is a lot of people have  either bulk or single cell rna-seq  information  that they want to integrate spatially  and so  single-star and i think has been great  because it tells you a lot about these  populations of cells but it doesn't  really tell you  where these cells are and so  we've developed methodologies to help  our customers uh do just that  um  so this is um alex warwick from the  garvin institute and  he's been working with triple negative  breast cancer  and so he performed um an analysis  with actually our whole transcriptome  atlas assay um where he was looking at  different  cell types in the tls structures  within the triple negative breast  cancers and so  just to give you the high level overview  he took  his single cell rna-seq signatures  because you can identify these very  clear populations of cells  um he then um created rois here in the  middle  um and then segmented on pancetta  keratin and then  um the stromal environment so really  everything else that it wasn't  tumor and then using these signatures  he was able to deconvolute the data  and understand the composition of each  of these segments and so  you could actually tell which cells were  in each of these segments  in these two regions and then across his  tissues in general  so it's a really good way to kind of  integrate both your single cell data  whether it's in cancer or neuro or any  other  tissue and take your single cell data  and then  use it in your  genomex dsp experiment so  with that i want to thank everybody for  your attention and interest  um you know feel free to reach out with  any questions  um and um you know you can find this  single cell decon on bio archives the  algorithm that we've just published  um or you know more information  about genomics and the cancer  transcription atlas  by emailing genomics management.com  thank you
 

